<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Cellar WatchPaths LaunchAgent — XACA-0578
  ==========================================
  Passive watcher that detects when Homebrew upgrades the aiteamforge formula
  in the Cellar and fires the cellar-watch-trigger.sh script to refresh the
  runtime working-dir copy at $AITEAMFORGE_DIR.

  PROBLEM THIS SOLVES:
  After `brew upgrade aiteamforge`, the Cellar is updated but the working-dir
  copy at $AITEAMFORGE_DIR/lcars-ui/ stays stale. This agent detects the
  Cellar mutation and chains aiteamforge upgrade --non-interactive to sync
  the working-dir copy.

  (a) WHY WE WATCH THE CELLAR PARENT DIR, NOT THE OPT SYMLINK:
  The Homebrew opt path (/opt/homebrew/opt/aiteamforge) is a SYMLINK that
  gets retargeted on upgrade. launchd WatchPaths holds a kernel event
  descriptor on the INODE of the original symlink target — when brew
  atomically retargets the symlink to a new versioned Cellar dir, launchd
  does NOT reliably detect the change because the inode it was watching no
  longer backs the name. The Cellar parent directory
  (/opt/homebrew/Cellar/aiteamforge) is a REAL DIRECTORY whose directory
  entries are mutated in-place: brew creates a new versioned subdir (e.g.
  Cellar/aiteamforge/0.12.6/) inside it, which bumps the parent dir's mtime.
  WatchPaths fires reliably because it's an in-place mutation, not a swap.

  (b) WHY ThrottleInterval IS 60s (not 30s like lcars-watch):
  The lcars-watch agent uses 30s to debounce the burst of dozens of `cp`
  calls during an in-place lcars-ui refresh. The Cellar parent dir, by
  contrast, sees only ONE or TWO directory entry mutations per brew upgrade
  (new versioned subdir created; old versioned subdir removed). There is no
  per-file burst. We use 60s — double lcars-watch's value — to ensure that
  in the uninstall+reinstall scenario (two separate mtime bumps arriving
  seconds apart), both events are coalesced into a single trigger fire. The
  60s window lets the first event (uninstall) and any immediately-following
  second event (reinstall start) coalesce, so the trigger only runs once the
  Cellar is fully settled.

  (c) UNINSTALL-RACE GUARD (trigger script responsibility):
  When the user runs `brew uninstall aiteamforge`, launchd fires this watcher
  because the Cellar parent dir's mtime changes. At that moment the formula
  is absent and calling `aiteamforge upgrade --non-interactive` directly
  would fail confusingly. We invoke a dedicated trigger script
  ({{CELLAR_WATCH_TRIGGER}}) instead of calling aiteamforge directly. The
  trigger script performs three guards before chaining the upgrade:
    Guard 1 — brew is on PATH
    Guard 2 — formula is installed (`brew list aiteamforge`)
    Guard 3 — aiteamforge CLI is reachable
  If any guard fails the script exits 0 (non-error) with a log entry
  explaining why it skipped the upgrade.

  (d) DOWNSTREAM CHAIN — NO RECURSION RISK:
  cellar-watch fires → cellar-watch-trigger.sh runs →
    aiteamforge upgrade --non-interactive runs →
      writes to $AITEAMFORGE_DIR/lcars-ui/ →
        lcars-watch fires → aiteamforge restart lcars
  cellar-watch and lcars-watch watch DISJOINT filesystem subtrees:
    cellar-watch: $(brew --prefix)/Cellar/aiteamforge/  (Homebrew Cellar)
    lcars-watch:  $AITEAMFORGE_DIR/lcars-ui/             (working-dir copy)
  The upgrade step writes only to $AITEAMFORGE_DIR, NOT to the Cellar.
  The LCARS restart step writes nothing to either watched path.
  There is no feedback loop. The chain is a linear DAG.

  NO KeepAlive  — this is a trigger, not a persistent daemon.
  NO RunAtLoad  — that would fire the trigger on every login, defeating the point.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.aiteamforge.cellar-watch</string>

    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <!-- Use absolute path to the trigger script: launchd PATH is minimal
             and does not include Homebrew bin by default. {{CELLAR_WATCH_TRIGGER}}
             is substituted by the installer to the full path of
             cellar-watch-trigger.sh. The trigger script (not aiteamforge
             directly) is invoked so it can apply the uninstall-race guards
             described in comment (c) above before chaining the upgrade. -->
        <string>{{CELLAR_WATCH_TRIGGER}}</string>
    </array>

    <!--
      WatchPaths: launchd fires when the directory entry (mtime) of this path
      changes. {{BREW_CELLAR_DIR}} is substituted at install time to:
        Apple Silicon: /opt/homebrew/Cellar/aiteamforge
        Intel:         /usr/local/Cellar/aiteamforge
      Watching the parent Cellar directory (not the opt symlink) is deliberate —
      see comment (a) above.
    -->
    <key>WatchPaths</key>
    <array>
        <string>{{BREW_CELLAR_DIR}}</string>
    </array>

    <!--
      ThrottleInterval: minimum seconds between successive job launches.
      Set to 60s (vs. lcars-watch's 30s) to coalesce the uninstall+reinstall
      double-fire scenario into a single trigger. See comment (b) above.
    -->
    <key>ThrottleInterval</key>
    <integer>60</integer>

    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>{{LOG_DIR}}/cellar-watch.log</string>

    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>{{LOG_DIR}}/cellar-watch.log</string>

    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
    <dict>
        <!-- Include both Apple Silicon and Intel Homebrew prefixes so
             the trigger script can locate brew regardless of architecture. -->
        <key>PATH</key>
        <string>/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin</string>
        <key>HOME</key>
        <string>{{HOME_DIR}}</string>
        <key>AITEAMFORGE_DIR</key>
        <string>{{AITEAMFORGE_DIR}}</string>
    </dict>
</dict>
</plist>
